  ],
  "text": "A perfect memory is not a superpower. It is a diagnosis. You assume forgetting is your brain failing. It is your brain choosing. Connections you stop using get pruned away deliberately, to keep the useful ones clear. People who cannot forget exist. Their day is interrupted by decades of detail they did not ask for. They struggle to think in general terms, because every example arrives whole. But the unsettling part is what happens to the memories you do keep. Pulling a memory up does not read it. It reopens it. And when it goes back down, it goes back changed by the room you are standing in now. So the story you have told the most times is the one furthest from what happened. Your clearest memories are the ones you have edited the most. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
